#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157298 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157102 :question/id #uuid "189d117b-b510-59dd-8340-a84bd1c08472" :question/math-academy-id "e-7409" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4e47cfcf3da66707e6bc898d099868bfbd6d5163dcd38d47e4c79b2180e89ec6.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^2+y^2=1$ and the line $x=-1,$ as shown above. Express the following double integral in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D xy \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\pi,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/76/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564599 :question-generator/id #uuid "001b5085-188d-59d6-8776-23528643ad2d" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186157298.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ff2307-bdea-53b3-88e5-97b96d5ad36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489749} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4cd776-9de6-538f-9d94-7a3efcb04fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489772} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4aa5b4-4bf8-52ba-bb0c-de13688b61cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489774} {:answer/id #uuid "c761b339-ab83-5bb8-80db-4cc0d6bebede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489946} {:answer/id #uuid "cce7b078-805e-5d09-a2f3-9ad87e8072c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce7b078-805e-5d09-a2f3-9ad87e8072c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489969} :answer-field/id #uuid "d25f125e-94ad-5225-b5e5-f24049a7a3e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155925}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d182d5c-4098-5732-b594-84e4e6e5bea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-83789" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b694a0907a5cb1c33d44c921b01b8e351059ce3e695485d79b72051f7ed764a.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the line $y=1,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/68/68f70968b0dd256159dbfdcbfde4f2f63578237db1770e019c8fb19a5a638eaa.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a268e5-f9a7-5175-82f6-429e900d7180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394560} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8c7c83-2673-55cc-b80f-82dd032a52b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394561} {:answer/id #uuid "781c89a4-4fc2-5fdd-81c9-1fe0439559bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394562} {:answer/id #uuid "c04067e2-1051-59f7-ba78-7d685ba5c6ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394563} {:answer/id #uuid "7e63b9da-492f-5a21-ae92-0344b2806a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a268e5-f9a7-5175-82f6-429e900d7180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394560} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e89d210-e453-5f03-84ad-9efe39e4cea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394559}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95f91c79-4c92-5753-8c02-6dfc6c0682b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83980" :question/problem "![](images/92/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and the line $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/37/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ac000c-1758-5e85-9be7-c02c06f56199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275539} {:answer/id #uuid "167a7194-5754-55f4-8e86-e2579f03ffce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275686} {:answer/id #uuid "365bc493-05f6-5b56-921d-bd416a928820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275962} {:answer/id #uuid "9b303eed-2f56-5daf-b733-6f11cc63c4e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276869} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6cc47b-0553-5b4a-8ebe-c0cfd59c1221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06ac000c-1758-5e85-9be7-c02c06f56199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275539} :answer-field/id #uuid "0852c563-c2ff-5b18-be16-1d7ac03b0110" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f11e52b-1477-5101-9ca1-e393f690fd0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161101" :question/problem "![](images/ae/aed70cc2e5495fcfd9f42ff5d79c3371fb17b62e12ab1909c7738071e59f1a23.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=x+2,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(-1,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2 & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ r(r-2(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).$\n\n- For $y=x+2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/b9/b9c2cd48302ef8d4e2d5e65a56d19506600c5e6552a4d9a1ef7d578a09a21557.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\le r\\le 2(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d8f91d3-c985-5ff9-a7d5-30ebbe848335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352276} {:answer/id #uuid "616be36f-20a7-5594-9222-5abdb16a236b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352438} {:answer/id #uuid "79385752-aec6-56af-a9f5-88f94e3cb797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352624} {:answer/id #uuid "9294ec35-9104-50c2-abc4-83106e810f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352836} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ba7cbd-8617-5aa0-9cf3-e77cd9c04b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79385752-aec6-56af-a9f5-88f94e3cb797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352624} :answer-field/id #uuid "379a280a-d1e2-57b2-b66e-8dfa34c9f758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353909}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5f310f9-e63f-56fe-abce-21ad9b7c4099" :question/math-academy-id "q-85065" :question/problem "![](images/15/15e58073c87bd249473950680a49e8fc4cb4be94277a7001c0ab9f96ab3a46df.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(-2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-3 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-3,-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/92/928c6dc79f44a2185ccf217c0cf9210630b6554a698f805ef2c77500f9ddc76b.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3925f952-8784-505e-bc36-b8e351bf796c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352095} {:answer/id #uuid "44d4d705-f224-5e7f-8e88-c0b5b262b904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352201} {:answer/id #uuid "9c55e4d5-332d-5270-a33b-b9ec0198d01e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352903} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f049cb-da7f-5369-ad4a-049c0ca77cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352945} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5aa41a-063a-567d-a29d-297b81942c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f049cb-da7f-5369-ad4a-049c0ca77cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352945} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f26d60-7625-57c8-9507-f3b0235b104b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354129}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d05c396-81e6-5aa1-b630-71a86aa552fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-85067" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc35d0362ef95d11402ad44cded01abf46686d3088e9d852bd0b9a7ba2454e16.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the line $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/ca/ca09f228fc218ef1b57196c6b6b1e740c10173b7cd38b1c27407726eccd783f1.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04830521-3355-5cb5-969c-1650b3f830fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351611} {:answer/id #uuid "100cff3b-1934-5849-b10d-7ff26075848e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{r}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351704} {:answer/id #uuid "148a4d56-41a6-57ea-804c-a991e5584c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351745} {:answer/id #uuid "82ffbdc0-51d3-5215-90bd-435914ccdf5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352712} {:answer/id #uuid "f86b2a50-5585-5203-b724-06d25c9c5e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82ffbdc0-51d3-5215-90bd-435914ccdf5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352712} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b9760e9-c40f-5ec5-866a-aa3fc636ddf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354088}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a399d3dc-47f2-5985-b430-d143a3ff87d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85182" :question/problem "![](images/51/51439321ae9d3760eb48d54351dbd519efe290c77d9197ca55aa590ce79e91a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =0,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/bf/bf2d20acbb93d3c61e9890a49d1c15a08c2b4e18b474da421bded7cf3dafc297.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{tan}\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f764ef66-80ba-5dd4-95f0-f40227cffaa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389141} {:answer/id #uuid "050352de-c1bd-5d67-9579-ebdbd025a455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389142} {:answer/id #uuid "482b7783-c529-524c-a098-a22acb66ee43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389143} {:answer/id #uuid "92fa62f8-7504-56f9-8cec-7b7eb2d858e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389144} {:answer/id #uuid "71e856e7-0056-5715-808a-11df70aa0fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92fa62f8-7504-56f9-8cec-7b7eb2d858e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389144} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b233718-f0ae-5430-bf3b-0ba9d8e47c07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389140}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f6d9f66-8b90-5e43-ac23-53d484fde86e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161100" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6ddc0b2138025b07ffdb7bf34afabeba578400b194922bec7a733cc9246c7c0f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=2-x,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(1,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2 & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ r(r-2(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$\n\n- For $y=2-x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-x \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/58/587c299c1bdf89061be205cf185d6335fba865d2e30c81cac5b9d223fc84b486.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\le r\\le 2(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ),\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "573018e5-a9fd-5e40-b891-d55300a84f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394663} {:answer/id #uuid "69c19221-15de-5240-b2da-bdd72a80b675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394664} {:answer/id #uuid "53a33246-e2dd-5eab-a0f9-7c365f453fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-4\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394665} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef2c7d7-f430-597f-a5c1-e3fbdee731e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394666} {:answer/id #uuid "dddf94f1-c50a-5fda-a1fd-b4ca42e1b905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dddf94f1-c50a-5fda-a1fd-b4ca42e1b905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394667} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d28e52c-d55e-5dd4-93bd-104296832afd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394662}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bddde3a-859e-58d0-9562-df6a6a576260" :question/math-academy-id "q-85062" :question/problem "![](images/ac/acb2f50d01df6ebec6003cc7b9c944b759113b9f364455fb62eede389b335229.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-3 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-\\sqrt{3},-3)$ and $(\\sqrt{3},-3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/59/5991463a56d8358164d32b15f73e938573463d8d706d2b9c8fc06803326a5f0f.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta \\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055b0e6b-d664-5538-b782-3dfbe82f8ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424297} {:answer/id #uuid "320a2c42-e7e8-5a89-b8ed-772fad2d8845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424302} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20bf88-0220-51b3-8e06-d32a2b7d8220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424305} {:answer/id #uuid "776ef258-4be4-56d1-84fa-73c7f0f2fd04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424309} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6a190c-ce1c-504a-9a43-1fa603eb0266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "055b0e6b-d664-5538-b782-3dfbe82f8ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424297} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc66dd31-0a57-5231-b848-a0871bc42f3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424326}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78459587-308f-5867-9175-f728a41ccebc" :question/math-academy-id "q-85181" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3d5542933934102149c58153c3108d55d9c615e991db8aca8d8fc1e60837917f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\sqrt{3},3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/49/4903ca2ba8cde3f7874e10998a0741035ec7b2dea69a7804eaca64cb552fc572.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}}{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }r{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "164a2e88-b870-534a-838f-cf3c412c7a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424299} {:answer/id #uuid "466e97c1-fc77-5fb7-94db-decc56abd8f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424304} {:answer/id #uuid "54526e04-2273-5cf8-9d70-fdb80efe57d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424306} {:answer/id #uuid "84775023-9e2b-50b6-8361-4921f16d83bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424312} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a5a807-5f29-57e4-ac91-cb6041b0cd30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1a5a807-5f29-57e4-ac91-cb6041b0cd30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424320} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f3ffa13-5a50-5681-afc0-a669c98e966e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424322}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97691a5c-96a1-5dc1-b545-4fdb3272878f" :question/math-academy-id "q-83979" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a51719e208dafaa32d7f28c338885313942513493bd9fe0392b1ef06004e91e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the line $x=1,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,dA\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(1,-1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/43/4371a691b81b2ed1cb5d3468f68cd24f41e8a6b87d378a3206238d58e61791f0.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x+y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709e5a2e-54c5-5f5c-b473-eb287ff2a6d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433769} {:answer/id #uuid "8e198036-a474-5058-bfdb-866d7d95cca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433771} {:answer/id #uuid "bd29b008-2b41-5c5a-b870-fc78e9661939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433774} {:answer/id #uuid "d79f9304-175d-5676-a985-65b66bd79d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433776} {:answer/id #uuid "df3f0225-7ec2-5288-9ed6-196d002eb9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d79f9304-175d-5676-a985-65b66bd79d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433776} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3248d29-8c2f-5b6e-bca3-612d18b52286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433778}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31312c0a-f5f5-5c45-80ec-b73eead72363" :question/math-academy-id "q-83975" :question/problem "![](images/37/37a6eb13f8a6f6ab46040495894dedeec4951b4814aad19f45bfbd4ed7040767.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/89/897028515a29fdc4174d2a101439a81a30895db41ac3d29f311d53e0e6a79e29.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}]}
